Reason: build the answer array as a genuine ragged 1-D object array. get_batch relies on np.array(self.kg.answer, dtype=object) giving one entry per query, each a list of true answers, but numpy only does that when the lists differ in length. Where every query has exactly one answer it builds a 2-D (n, 1) array, answer[i] becomes an ndarray, and using it as an index raises IndexError. Metafam is the one graph in this suite with a single answer per query, and it fails outright without this. Nothing changes for any dataset that was already working: the object array holds the same lists. Applies on top of 0003.

diff --git a/src/data_loader.py b/src/data_loader.py
index bfe6571..0fda3f9 100644
--- a/src/data_loader.py
+++ b/src/data_loader.py
@@ -21,7 +21,18 @@ class DataLoader:
         if mode == 'train':
             return self.kg.data[batch_idx], self.kg.entity_num
         else:
-            query, answer = np.array(self.kg.query), np.array(self.kg.answer, dtype=object)
+            # np.array(list_of_lists, dtype=object) only produces the ragged
+            # 1-D array this code expects when the lists have *different*
+            # lengths. When every query has exactly one answer numpy builds a
+            # 2-D (n, 1) object array instead, answer[i] is then an ndarray
+            # rather than a list, and the line below fails with
+            #   IndexError: arrays used as indices must be of integer type
+            # Of the 41 graphs in this suite exactly one, Metafam, has a
+            # single answer for every query, which is why the shipped
+            # datasets never trip it.
+            query = np.array(self.kg.query)
+            answer = np.empty(len(self.kg.answer), dtype=object)
+            answer[:] = self.kg.answer
             subs = query[batch_idx, 0]
             rels = query[batch_idx, 1]
             objs = np.zeros((len(batch_idx), self.kg.entity_num))
